---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';

const pageSlug = 'security';
const articleLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'How Vireault encrypts your files on Android',
  description:
    'Plain-language security model for Vireault: Argon2id, AES-256-GCM, encrypted index, backups, and what encryption does not protect against.',
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Vireault' },
  datePublished: '2026-11-05',
  dateModified: '2026-11-05',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://vireault.app/security',
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How Vireault encrypts your files"
  description="How Vireault protects Android vault files with Argon2id and AES-256-GCM, what stays local, and the limits we do not overclaim."
  path="/security"
  pageSlug={pageSlug}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={articleLd}
  article={{ published: '2026-11-05', modified: '2026-11-05' }}
>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { label: 'Security' }]} />
    <article class="section prose">
      <h1>How Vireault encrypts your files</h1>
      <p>
        <strong>Short answer:</strong> Vireault encrypts vault contents on your Android phone with
        AES-256-GCM. A random 256-bit master key does the encryption. Your PIN, 3×3 pattern or
        passphrase unlocks that key through Argon2id. We run no vault servers and hold no keys, so
        we cannot open your vault or reset a forgotten secret. This page states what ships today and
        what encryption does <em>not</em> protect against.
      </p>

      <h2>Design goals</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>On-device only.</strong> Vault bytes stay in app-private storage unless you export a backup file you control.</li>
        <li><strong>No account.</strong> There is no Vireault login, cloud sync or remote recovery.</li>
        <li><strong>Authenticated encryption.</strong> AES-256-GCM both encrypts and detects tampering of vault blobs.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limits.</strong> We describe weak spots instead of marketing “military-grade” with no detail.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What happens when you unlock</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>You enter a PIN, draw a 3×3 pattern, or type a passphrase.</li>
        <li>Argon2id stretches that secret with a per-vault salt (default about 64&nbsp;MiB of memory on phones that can spare it; 32&nbsp;MiB on tighter devices).</li>
        <li>The derived key unwraps a random master key stored in the vault header (AES-256-GCM wrap).</li>
        <li>That master key decrypts the encrypted index and file blobs while the vault stays open.</li>
        <li>When the vault locks, the in-app master key material the app controls is wiped so further reads fail closed.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        New vaults use Argon2id. Older vaults created under a legacy PBKDF2 header format are upgraded
        on a successful unlock. Details live in the open
        <a href="https://github.com/Dixonchoi22/vaultdroid/blob/main/SECURITY.md">SECURITY.md</a>
        in the app repository.
      </p>

      <Cta
        pageSlug={pageSlug}
        heading="Try on-device encryption without an account"
        body="Free plan: 1 vault and 50 items. Pro from $1.99/week, $19.99/year (3-day trial), or $29.99 lifetime."
      />

      <h2>What is encrypted on disk</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>File contents</strong> — AES-256-GCM. Newer items use segmented STREAM-style blobs bound to the item id so truncation, reordering or swapping blobs fails authentication.</li>
        <li><strong>Index / names</strong> — the list of items (names, types, sizes) is encrypted, so cleartext titles are not sitting beside the blobs.</li>
        <li><strong>Thumbnails</strong> — encrypted and bound to their item when present.</li>
        <li><strong>Backups</strong> — an exported backup is its own encrypted file, protected by a generated recovery code (not your everyday PIN), with authenticated segments so tampering is detected.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Related product pages:
        <a href="/features/encryption">encryption feature</a>,
        <a href="/features/offline">offline design</a>,
        <a href="/features/backup-restore">backup and restore</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Day-to-day protections</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Auto-lock when the app goes to the background, and after about five minutes idle.</li>
        <li>Screenshot / screen-recording blocking on vault screens (<code>FLAG_SECURE</code>).</li>
        <li>Escalating on-device lockout after repeated wrong unlocks.</li>
        <li>Viewing media and PDFs without writing decrypted temp files to storage.</li>
        <li>Optional delete of gallery originals after a successful import (Android’s normal delete; cloud copies are yours to remove).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Vireault does <em>not</em> protect against</h2>
      <p>Encryption is not magic. These limits are intentional or inherent — please read them before trusting irreplaceable media to any vault.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Lost secrets.</strong> If you forget your unlock secret and lose backup recovery
          material, nobody can reconstruct the files — including us. There is no email reset of vault
          contents.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Short PINs or patterns against offline copies.</strong> On-device lockout does not
          apply if someone with root or forensic access copies vault files off the phone. A 6-digit
          PIN has one million combinations; Argon2id makes each guess expensive but cannot enlarge
          the search space. Prefer a long passphrase for stronger protection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A compromised or rooted OS while the vault is unlocked.</strong> Malware with
          accessibility rights, a malicious screen overlay, or full device compromise is out of scope.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Existence leakage.</strong> Someone inspecting app storage can still see that vaults
          exist, vault directory names, approximate sizes and counts. Encryption hides contents, not
          that you installed a vault.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Guaranteed flash erasure.</strong> Secure delete overwrites then unlinks; flash wear
          levelling can still leave unreachable remnants. Remnants of vault data remain ciphertext.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Files you leave outside the vault.</strong> Gallery, messaging and cloud backups can
          keep copies after import unless you remove them yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Features not shipped yet.</strong> No biometrics, decoy vaults, cloud sync or in-app
          camera today. Folders are coming soon. We do not claim a third-party audit on this page.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How this differs from “hide” apps</h2>
      <p>
        Many Android “photo vault” products primarily hide or rename files, show ads, or rely on an
        account and vendor cloud. Hiding is not the same as authenticated encryption. If you are
        choosing an app after a scare, also read
        <a href="/guides/photo-vault-lost-photos-android">what to do when a photo vault loses photos</a>,
        the <a href="/guides/keepsafe-alternative-android-no-account">Keepsafe alternative without an account</a>
        guide, and the <a href="/guides/secure-photo-vault-android">secure photo vault checklist</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy and purchases</h2>
      <p>
        Vault content is not uploaded to us. Purchases go through Google Play Billing. See the
        <a href="/privacy.html">privacy policy</a> for data practices, and
        <a href="/pricing">pricing</a> for free vs Pro limits (free: 1 vault / 50 items; Pro
        $1.99/week, $19.99/year with a 3-day trial, or $29.99 lifetime).
      </p>

      <h2>Keep reading</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/features/encryption">AES-256 encryption feature page</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/encrypt-files-on-android">Encrypting files on Android</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/keepsafe-alternative-android-no-account">Keepsafe alternative without an account</a></li>
        <li><a href="/faq">FAQ</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/Dixonchoi22/vaultdroid/blob/main/SECURITY.md">Full SECURITY.md (technical)</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
    <Cta pageSlug={pageSlug} />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
